{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel came, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say <b>all the congregation</b>, and why the statement <b>children of Israel</b> was not sufficient — for presumably they are all the congregation? Now, according to what has preceded for us from their words (Numbers Rabbah, end of parashat Balak), when Israel is on the level of the upright and the righteous they are called \"children of Israel,\" and when they are not in the fitting category they are called \"people,\" as it says (Numbers 14:11): ״עַד אָנָה יְנַאֲצֻנִי הָעָם הַזֶּה״ — and there are many similar cases. And many times Scripture calls them \"the people, the children of Israel,\" even though among them are many who are not worthy of this category, and Scripture intends the fit majority. But here Scripture informs us that all the congregation was in this category — that they would be called \"children of Israel\" — which is the statement <b>and the children of Israel came, all the congregation</b>, meaning, all of them are called by the name Israel in the category of the upright and the righteous. And this corresponds to the words of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, who said, and this is their language: ״כָּל הָעֵדָה״ — a complete congregation." "<b>And they</b>, of blessed memory — it appears that they interpreted the word ״כָּל הָעֵדָה״ itself as indicating completeness and permanence, and there are seventy faces to the Torah. And the intention behind this declaration is for three reasons: First, because Scripture states nearby the episode of the waters of Meribah, and they, of blessed memory, said (Deuteronomy Rabbah, parashat Va'etchanan) that Moses was punished on their account, for he said to them ״שִׁמְעוּ נָא הַמּוֹרִים״; therefore he prefaced by informing you that all the congregation were righteous, and this was the reason Moses was punished for calling them rebels. Second, to magnify the merit of Miriam, peace be upon her, for even though all the congregation were righteous, their merit did not suffice to maintain for them the well of water that existed in the merit of Miriam (Ta'anit 9a). Third, according to their words, of blessed memory (Yalkut Shimoni 593), who said that Israel did not go to perform kindness for Miriam, and when they assembled against Moses and against Aaron, Aaron thought they had come to perform kindness for Miriam, but they had come only to contend with them, as stated in their words, of blessed memory. From this one might have grounds to suspect Israel that they were not then in a fit state, and this is your proof — that they did not perform kindness for the prophetess. Therefore he prefaced and informed you that all the congregation were righteous, who would be called by the name Israel; and the reason they did not perform kindness, Scripture states nearby: ״וַתָּמָת שָׁם מִרְיָם וַתִּקָּבֵר שָׁם״, and they said in the Gemara (Mo'ed Katan 28a), and this is their language: ״וַתָּמָת״ etc. — adjacent to death is burial, end quote. And for this reason they did not bestir themselves to come to perform kindness, and after a short time had passed, once they knew, the wrath of thirst leapt upon them, and this is what Scripture states adjacent to the statement ״וַתִּקָּבֵר שָׁם״ — ״וְלֹא הָיָה מַיִם״ etc." "<b>And the people stayed in Kadesh.</b> This comes to inform us that they tarried there many days, as it says (Deuteronomy 1:46): ״וַתֵּשְׁבוּ בְקָדֵשׁ יָמִים רַבִּים״. The meaning of <b>וַיֵּשֶׁב</b> is the language of tarrying." "<b>And she died there, etc.</b> One needs to know the reason for His saying <b>there</b>, and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted (Mo'ed Katan 28a) that they did not delay her, and this interpretation is heard from the statement <b>and she was buried there</b>. And it appears that since He mentioned death, He was concerned for the honor of the righteous woman, for they said (Berakhot 18a) that the righteous in their death are called living; therefore He was precise in saying <b>there</b>, meaning, there — there is where she died, but she did not remain among them except in another place she exists among the righteous, for the righteous are likened before the Holy One, blessed be He, to gems placed in a box: when He desires one of them, He takes it out of the box and sets it in one of His ornaments. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted (Mo'ed Katan 28a) ״וַתָּמָת שָׁם״ by way of gezerah shavah, to say that she too died by a kiss, and there are seventy faces to the Torah."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The citation \"Numbers Rabbah, end of parashat Balak\" is given as in the Hebrew; the referenced teaching about the names 'Israel' vs. 'people' appears in Midrashic literature.", 1 "The citation \"Yalkut Shimoni 593\" is rendered with the numeral as given in the Hebrew (תשס״ג = 593 in Hebrew numerals)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:11:22.581998526Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}